body:
I was going to add both the signer and move to, but the move to doesn't
work due to Move requiring that resources are defined in the module in
which you use global storage :/. Probably need to explore a native
function for this.

A resource account is used to manage resources independent of an account
managed by a user.  Some applications might include managing a Dao and
the resources associated with the Dao or a liquidity pool to create new
liquidity pool coins without requiring the original account owner to
setup the resources. While one could theoretically manage a lot of this
via capabilities, the `move_to` semantics insist on a signer and cannot
work with capabilities due to Move requirements that `move_to` be
executed on a resource within the same module that defines that
resource.

As a small example:
```
let (signer, cap) = create_resource_account(&source);
let lp = LiquidityPool { signer_cap: cap, ... };
move_to(&signer, lp);
```

Later on during a coin registration:
```
public fun add_coin<X, Y>(lp: &LP, x: Coin<x>, y: Coin<y>) {
   if(!exists<LiquidityCoin<X, Y>(LP::Address(lp), LiquidityCoin<X, Y>)) {
       let mint, burn = Coin::initialize<LiquidityCoin<X, Y>>(...);
       move_to(&create_signer_with_capability(&lp.cap), LiquidityCoin<X, Y>{ mint, burn });
   }
   ...
}
```

Closes: #1367

This PR introduces as the matching counterpart to `std::bcs` the module `aptos_std::from_bcs`. It seems to be appropriate to tie the byte conversions together under the `bcs` label because that is how we have implemented them.

All safe conversions from primitive types + Strings bytes to values are supported. Naming is as in `from_bcs::to_<type>`. Example:

```
use std::bcs;
use aptos_std::from_bcs;

assert!(from_bcs::to_address(bcs::to_bytes(&@0xabcdef)) == @0xabcdef;
```

Also the friend native `from_bytes` is now defined in `from_bcs` instead of `any`.

## Description
```
module aptos_framework::signing_data {
    enum SigningData has copy, drop {
        V1 { digest: vector<u8>, authenticator: vector<u8> },
    }

    #[test_only]
    public fun create_signing_data(digest: vector<u8>): SigningData {
        SigningData::V1 { digest }
    }

    public fun digest(signing_data: &SigningData): &vector<u8> {
        &signing_data.digest
    }

    public fun authenticator(signing_data: &SigningData): &vector<u8> {
        &signing_data.authenticator
    }
}
```
a function with the following signature can be used to authorize account..
```
/// The native function to dispatch customized move authentication function.
 native fun dispatchable_authenticate(
        account: signer,
        signing_data: SigningData,
        function: &FunctionInfo
    ): signer;
```

* [prover] Universal state quantification and other bug fixes in dynamic dispatch handling

This are a bunch of fixes coming out of trying to make automated-market-maker example work, in prep for the potential FMCAD paper.

The AMM (amm_example.move) is an UniSwap type of pool with a dynamic configurable pricing strategy, which is invoked via dynamic dispatch. There are multiple conditions for a valid strategy, expressed as data invariants:

```
struct PricingStrategy(|address, u64, u64, u64|u64) has store, copy, drop;
spec PricingStrategy {
    modifies_of<self.0> *;

    // No-abort: the pricing function must never abort, for any inputs.
    invariant forall S in *, client: address, r_in: u64, r_out: u64, amt: u64:
            S |~ !aborts_of<self.0>(client, r_in, r_out, amt);

    // Safety: output never exceeds the output reserve.
    invariant forall S in *, client: address, r_in: u64, r_out: u64, amt: u64:
            S.. |~ result_of<self.0>(client, r_in, r_out, amt) <= r_out;

    // Monotonicity: more input yields at least as much output.
    invariant forall S in *, client: address, r_in: u64, r_out: u64, a1: u64, a2: u64:
            S.. |~ a1 <= a2 ==>
                result_of<self.0>(client, r_in, r_out, a1)
                    <= result_of<self.0>(client, r_in, r_out, a2);

    // Constant product preservation: the product of reserves never decreases.
    invariant forall S in *, client: address, r_in: u64, r_out: u64, amt: u64:
            S.. |~ (r_in + amt) * (r_out - result_of<self.0>(client, r_in, r_out, amt)) >= r_in * r_out;

}
```

These invariants are (a) injected at usage set, allowing the caller of the stored function to reason about results (b) verified at pack or mutation side.

The general approach worked to some extend before but had multiple issues:

- Missing state quantification: we need be able to express `forall S in *: S |~ !aborts_of<f>(x)`. Without an explicit state label context, the predicate would otherwise
  be evaluated in the current state. But we want to express something about `f` which holds for every state. This leads to a language extension in the frontend for
  supporting quantifiers over state labels. With the current architecture, this has a bit of cascading consequences, nammely that state labels need to become typed
  objects, so we have new type `Type::StateDomain` for those labels.
- Timeouts: a lot of attempts to optimized verification behavior (verification for the example takes long, and times out without newly added
  flag `--split-vcs-by-assert`). There are some notes about the experiments in amm_example_notes.md.

* Address reviewer comments

Replaces lambda lifting for inline function calls, which cannot support
mutable reference captures, by inlining behavioral predicates at the
expansion sites. Together with spec function specialization and lemmas,
this makes higher-order inline functions like `for_each_ref` and `fold`
specifiable and verifiable for fully symbolic callers — including
lambdas which read and modify global state, and lambdas which
accumulate into captured variables.

Element-wise HOFs verify symbolic callers directly. The generic loop
invariant constrains the function parameter via behavioral predicates
(`invariant forall j in 0..i: ensures_of<f>(old(v)[j], v[j])` in
`for_each_mut`), which are substituted per caller by the lambda's
attached spec, or by a spec derived from its body:

```move
fun increment_all(v: &mut vector<u64>) {
    for_each_mut(v, |e| *e = *e + 1);
}
spec increment_all {
    requires forall i in 0..len(v): v[i] < MAX_U64;
    aborts_if false;
    ensures forall i in 0..len(v): v[i] == old(v)[i] + 1;
}
```

Accumulating HOFs use a recursive spec function over the function value
(`spec_fold`, in `fold`'s loop invariant), which is specialized per
lambda. A caller can state its spec directly in terms of `spec_fold`
with a literal lambda, which unifies with the specialization from the
expanded invariant — no further proof needed:

```move
fun sum(v: &vector<u64>): u64 {
    fold(v, 0, |acc, e| acc + *e)
}
spec sum {
    pragma aborts_if_is_partial;
    ensures result == spec_fold<u64, u64>(|acc, e| acc + e, v, 0, len(v));
}
```

Relating the fold to a user-defined recursion requires induction: one
inductive bridging lemma per (lambda, abstraction) pair, applied at any
caller. This gives exact abort and result conditions:

```move
spec fun spec_sum(v: vector<u64>, n: num): num {
    if (n == 0) 0 else spec_sum(v, n - 1) + v[n - 1]
}
spec lemma fold_is_sum(v: vector<u64>, n: u64) {
    requires n <= len(v);
    ensures spec_fold<u64, u64>(|acc, e| acc + e, v, 0, n) == spec_sum(v, n);
} proof {
    if (n > 0) { apply fold_is_sum(v, n - 1); }
}
spec sum {
    aborts_if spec_sum(v, len(v)) > MAX_U64;
    ensures result == spec_sum(v, len(v));
} proof {
    apply fold_is_sum(v, len(v));
    // + forall-apply instantiations for the abort direction
}
```

Lambdas may also modify global state. In a loop invariant, behavioral
predicates over such a lambda resolve the pre-state to `old(..)`
(function entry) and the post-state to the current state, with memory
effects projected to per-element facts; the `unchanged_of<f>(x)`
predicate provides the frame over the lambda's write footprint:

```move
inline fun for_each_addr(v: &vector<address>, f: |address|) {
    let i = 0;
    let n = vector::length(v);
    while (i < n) {
        f(*vector::borrow(v, i));
        i = i + 1;
    } spec {
        invariant i <= n;
        invariant forall j in 0..i: ensures_of<f>(v[j]);
        invariant forall j in 0..i: !aborts_of<f>(v[j]);
        invariant forall j in i..n: unchanged_of<f>(v[j]);
        invariant forall x: (forall j in 0..i: x != v[j]) ==> unchanged_of<f>(x);
    };
}

fun bump_all(v: &vector<address>) {
    for_each_addr(v, |a| { let r = &mut R[a]; r.v = r.v + 1; });
}
spec bump_all {
    requires forall i in 0..len(v), j in 0..len(v): i != j ==> v[i] != v[j];
    requires forall i in 0..len(v): exists<R>(v[i]) && R[v[i]].v < MAX_U64;
    aborts_if false;
    ensures forall i in 0..len(v): R[v[i]].v == old(R[v[i]].v) + 1;
}
```

Finally, a lambda may accumulate into captured variables: it is treated
as a fold with the mutable-capture tuple as implicit accumulator. The
new `folds_of<f>` predicate in the HOF's loop invariant states that `f`
has been folded once over each processed element; per caller, the
accumulator transformer is derived from the lambda body, the captures
are snapshotted at expansion entry, and the loop is characterized by a
specialized recursion which the caller can name by restating the
transformer:

```move
inline fun for_each_ref<T>(v: &vector<T>, f: |&T|) {
    let i = 0;
    let n = vector::length(v);
    while (i < n) {
        f(vector::borrow(v, i));
        i = i + 1;
    } spec {
        invariant i <= n && n == len(v);
        invariant folds_of<f>(v, i);
    };
}

fun sum_all(v: &vector<u64>): u64 {
    let sum = 0;
    for_each_ref(v, |e| sum = sum + *e);
    sum
}
spec sum_all {
    pragma aborts_if_is_partial;
    ensures result == spec_fold<u64, u64>(|acc, e| acc + e, v, 0, len(v));
}
```

Exact abort and result conditions compose via lemmas exactly as with
`fold`. The general form `folds_of<f>(|j| .., i)` covers index-aware,
paired, and reverse iteration (enumerate, zip); multiple captures use a
generated tuple-valued recursion, and coupled accumulation behind a
captured `&mut` struct reference is supported as the restatable idiom.

- Specs on inline functions are restricted to functions without
  function-typed parameters; those are verified standalone and retained
  at call sites if opaque. Specs on inline HOFs are rejected.
- Behavioral predicates over lambda arguments in in-body spec blocks
  (e.g. loop invariants) are substituted by the lambda's attached spec,
  or by a spec derived from its body via a new source-level WP analysis
  (move-model/src/spec_derivation.rs) covering imperative bodies,
  modular call summaries, global state effects, and mutable captures
  modeled as implicit accumulator parameters.
- Spec functions taking function parameters are specialized per lambda;
  specializations are unified across contexts by spec-equivalence of the
  lambdas, so literal lambdas in caller specs and lemmas denote the same
  specialization as the expansion.
- Two-state derived specs are anchored at the parameter's unique
  application site via state anchor operations; in loop invariants they
  are resolved against function entry and the current state, with
  whole-memory effects projected to per-element point facts. The
  `unchanged_of<f>` behavioral predicate supplies the frame from the
  derived write footprint.
- The `folds_of<f>` behavioral predicate characterizes capture-
  accumulating lambdas: derived transformer, capture snapshots at the
  expansion site, and a specialized recursion resolved against
  `std::vector::spec_fold`/`spec_fold_idx` by convention (same-shaped
  local declarations serve as fallback), with generated tuple-valued
  recursions for multiple captures and a precise error taxonomy at the
  boundaries (state-dependent accumulation, consuming iteration,
  parameter writes).
- `old(..)` over global state is now allowed in inline spec blocks and
  loop invariants (previously restricted to plain parameters).
- Applying a function value in a specification is an error in verify
  mode; behavioral predicates must be used.

Co-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

## Motivation
Implements an OCR3 interface that supports the latest OCR needs:

1. Multi-plugin support: Ability to configure & use multiple OCR plugins
under one contract interface
2. OCR3 plugin compatibility: interface changes (pulled from
liquiditymanager OCR3Abstract)
3. Simplified config digest handling: no longer re-compute config digest
/ no passing of on-chain config, since the source of truth data will
live in the Capability Registry

## Solution
**Focus**: the foucs of the PR is on the **interface** and structure
rather on the exact logic

* Implements a `MultiOCR3Base` abstract contract to match multi-plugin,
Capability Registry and OCR3 plugin interface expectations
* Converts `_transmit` to be internal - implementations are expected to
implement separate `transmit` functions per-DON, since each report logic
will be different, e.g. for CCIP:

```solidity
function transmitExecution(
  // same params as _transmit, except the donId
) {
  // ... handle reporting logic
  _transmit(
    s_transmitDonId,
    // (pass in remaining params from function args)
  );
}

function transmitCommit(
  // same params as _transmit, except the donId
) {
  // ... handle reporting logic
  _transmit(
     s_commitDonId,
     // (pass in remaining params from function args)
  );
}
```

* Sample implementation of `MultiOCR3Base`, with signature skipping
support & multi-DON configs

* Out of scope
  * Config validations & oracle updating logic
  * Transmit msg length handling for different transmit function cases
  * Gas / contract size optimizations

Depends on : https://github.com/smartcontractkit/chainlink/pull/13878

The price registry needs to conform to the KeystoneFeedsConsumer
interface in order to receive keystone price feed updates.

Implemented Keystones `IReciever` interface `onReport` function to
handle the following report type

```
struct ReceivedFeedReport {
    address Token;
    uint224 Price;
    uint32 Timestamp;
}
```

and storing the reported fee in `Internal.TimestampedPackedUint224` for
`s_usdPerToken` mapping

